{"content":[{"c":["CAIN (P",{"c":["erson"],"sourceClass":"lc","t":"span"},")"],"sourceClass":"h1","t":"p"},{"c":["First son of Adam and Eve, who became a tiller of the soil while his brother, Abel, was a keeper of sheep. Cain’s murder of Abel became proverbial of similarly violent and destructive sins (",{"href":"?bref=Jude.1.11","r":"Jude.1.11","rawR":"Jude.1.11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Jude.1.11","value":"Jude.1.11"}],"t":"ref","v":"Jude 1:11"},"). Each of the two brothers had brought a sacrifice to the Lord (",{"href":"?bref=Gen.4.3-4","r":"Gen.4.3-4","rawR":"Gen.4.3-4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.4.3-4","value":"Gen.4.3-4"}],"t":"ref","v":"Gn 4:3-4"},"). According to ",{"href":"?bref=Heb.11.4","r":"Heb.11.4","rawR":"Heb.11.4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Heb.11.4","value":"Heb.11.4"}],"t":"ref","v":"Hebrews 11:4"},", Abel had acted in faith by bringing a more acceptable sacrifice than that of Cain. The latter’s anger had flared against the divine rejection. In retaliation, he killed his brother, whose offering had been accepted (",{"href":"?bref=Gen.4.5-8","r":"Gen.4.5-8","rawR":"Gen.4.5-8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.4.5-8","value":"Gen.4.5-8"}],"t":"ref","v":"Gn 4:5-8"},"). In seeking a reason for Cain’s inappropriate violent reaction, biblical commentary simply says that he belonged to the evil one (",{"href":"?bref=1Jn.3.12","r":"1Jn.3.12","rawR":"1Jn.3.12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Jn.3.12","value":"1Jn.3.12"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Jn 3:12"},"). The Lord confronted Cain with his guilt, judged him, and pronounced a curse upon him, driving him out to the land of Nod, east of Eden (",{"href":"?bref=Gen.4.9-16","r":"Gen.4.9-16","rawR":"Gen.4.9-16","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.4.9-16","value":"Gen.4.9-16"}],"t":"ref","v":"Gn 4:9-16"},"). When he complained that his punishment was greater than he could bear and that someone would find him and kill him, the Lord placed a mark on Cain and promised to take sevenfold vengeance on anyone who dared to kill him."],"sourceClass":"fl","t":"p"},{"c":["In the land of Nod, Cain built a city and named it after his son Enoch (",{"href":"?bref=Gen.4.17","r":"Gen.4.17","rawR":"Gen.4.17","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.4.17","value":"Gen.4.17"}],"t":"ref","v":"Gn 4:17"},"). Through Enoch, Cain became the progenitor of a large family that during its early generations became tent-dwelling herdsmen, musicians, and fashioners of metal objects and implements (vv ",{"href":"?bref=Gen.4.18-22","r":"Gen.4.18-22","rawR":"Gen.4.18-22","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.4.18-22","value":"Gen.4.18-22"}],"t":"ref","v":"18-22"},")."],"sourceClass":"","t":"p"}],"format":"bsb-study-dictionary-v2","id":"CainPerson","provenance":{"archiveSha256":"10758ea00dbea7540f9337e43e59c8e4000295c1261e3426bab6f31c31dcff5b","release":"1.6","sourceCitationReferences":[{"anchorText":"Jude 1:11","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Jude.1.11","rawQueryValue":"Jude.1.11","sourceRefExact":"Jude.1.11"},{"anchorText":"Gn 4:3-4","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Gen.4.3-4","rawQueryValue":"Gen.4.3-4","sourceRefExact":"Gen.4.3-4"},{"anchorText":"Hebrews 11:4","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Heb.11.4","rawQueryValue":"Heb.11.4","sourceRefExact":"Heb.11.4"},{"anchorText":"Gn 4:5-8","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Gen.4.5-8","rawQueryValue":"Gen.4.5-8","sourceRefExact":"Gen.4.5-8"},{"anchorText":"1 Jn 3:12","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=1Jn.3.12","rawQueryValue":"1Jn.3.12","sourceRefExact":"1Jn.3.12"},{"anchorText":"Gn 4:9-16","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Gen.4.9-16","rawQueryValue":"Gen.4.9-16","sourceRefExact":"Gen.4.9-16"},{"anchorText":"Gn 4:17","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Gen.4.17","rawQueryValue":"Gen.4.17","sourceRefExact":"Gen.4.17"},{"anchorText":"18-22","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Gen.4.18-22","rawQueryValue":"Gen.4.18-22","sourceRefExact":"Gen.4.18-22"}],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"4e7d1c0dcec68976a6a3a3239e405a4db51c56108a94d9a8fec46f1c2f4edfcb","sourceItemName":"CainPerson","sourceItemOrder":9,"sourceMember":"Articles/C.xml","supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[]},"sourceAttributes":{"name":"CainPerson","product":"TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","typename":"Article"},"sourceTitleExact":"Cain (Person)","title":"Cain (Person)","version":"v2-a101d843a2f0cd3fc0c6"}